@@s001 How to answer at the oral
@@s002 <span>Exam</span>
@@s003 How to answer at the oral
@@s004 The course questions always come back in the same 6 types. For each one: the outline of the answer and a model sentence in English, to adapt. They hold for the written answers of the assignments, for the oral feedback and for the exam.
@@s005 The general outline (without preparation)
@@s006 Talking for 3 minutes on a question
@@s007 "A data race occurs when…". The course definitions, word for word: <a href="../teoria/basi-glossario.html">glossary</a>.
@@s008 <code>count++</code> with two threads covers race condition, lock, interleaving, data race and volatile.
@@s009 Write or draw the sequence <code>t1(1), t2(1), …</code> or the chain of HB pairs.
@@s010 "Therefore…". Answer yes/no when the question is yes/no.
@@s011 What the solution does <b>not</b> guarantee (volatile: no mutual exclusion; non-fair lock: starvation possible). It is the point that separates the high-grade answers.
@@s012 Type 1 · "Explain why your solution is correct"
@@s013 Exercises 1.1.4, 1.1.7, 1.2.3, 1.3.2. The text asks to use "critical sections, interleavings, race conditions, mutual exclusion".
@@s014 Outline
@@s015 The lines that read and write the shared state.
@@s016 At most one thread in the critical section.
@@s017 Those in which the operations of the two threads overlap.
@@s018 And why no others.
@@s019 Model sentence
@@s020 "The critical section is the read and write of <i>count</i> in <i>increment()</i>. We protect it with a ReentrantLock, calling <i>lock()</i> before it and <i>unlock()</i> in a finally block. The lock guarantees mutual exclusion: at most one thread executes the critical section at a time. Therefore no interleaving can place a read of one thread between the read and the write of the other, so no increment is lost. Each of the 20 million increments reads the value written by the previous one, and the only possible output is 20,000,000."
@@s021 Type 2 · "Is this output possible?"
@@s022 Exercises 1.2.2, 1.2.4, the minimum value of the counter.
@@s023 <b>If yes</b>: write a concrete interleaving, action by action, and show that it produces that output. One example is enough.
@@s024 <b>If no</b>: by contradiction. "Assume an interleaving produces it. Then … must happen before …, but … Contradiction." Here one example is not enough: you must cover <b>all</b> the interleavings.
@@s025 Model sentence
@@s026 "Yes. Consider the interleaving t1(1), t2(1), t1(2), t2(2): both threads read <i>count</i> = 0 before either writes, so both write 1 and one increment is lost. Hence the program can print 1."
@@s027 Type 3 · "Show, using the Java memory model, that…"
@@s028 Exercises 3.1–3.3. Here one does not speak of interleavings but of <b>happens-before</b>.
@@s029 The strategy in 6 steps
@@s030 variable access, synchronization, other.
@@s031 The same in all executions.
@@s032 If there are many, group them by "who takes the lock first".
@@s033 One per family of synchronization order.
@@s034 Only between different threads.
@@s035 For "yes" show the path of arrows; for "no" show that it does not exist. <a href="../teoria/l03-esercizio-31.html">Exercise 3.1 worked out</a>.
@@s036 Model sentence
@@s037 "t1(2) and t2(2) are conflicting actions: both write the non-volatile variable <i>count</i>. The only pairs relating t1 and t2 come from the thread start and termination rules, which relate them to the main thread only. Hence neither t1(2) → t2(2) nor t2(2) → t1(2) is in the happens-before order, in any execution, and the program contains a data race."
@@s038 Type 4 · "Is this class thread-safe?"
@@s039 Exercises 4.1.2, 4.2.2. The slides: "you must always cover these elements".
@@s040 Outline in English
@@s041 "<b>Class state</b>: the fields are … <b>Escaping</b>: all fields are private and no method returns a reference to them, so the state does not escape. <b>Safe publication</b>: … is final / has its default value / is volatile, so its initialization happens-before any access. <b>Immutability</b>: … never changes after construction. <b>Mutual exclusion</b>: every access to the mutable fields … holds the lock …, so all conflicting accesses are ordered by the monitor rule. Therefore there are no data races on the class state, and the class is thread-safe."
@@s042 If the class is <b>not</b> thread-safe: indicate the element that is missing and the two conflicting actions that are not ordered. <a href="../teoria/l04-thread-safety.html">Thread-safe classes</a>.
@@s043 Type 5 · "Can the program loop forever?" (visibility)
@@s044 Exercises 2.2, 3.3. The answer is almost always "yes, it is <b>possible</b>", even if when you run it you do not see it.
@@s045 Model sentence
@@s046 "Yes, it is possible. The write <i>x = 42</i> in main and the reads of <i>x</i> in t1 are not ordered by happens-before: there is no lock, no volatile variable and no join relating them. Therefore the Java memory model does not guarantee that t1 ever sees the new value, and the loop may run forever. Declaring <i>x</i> volatile fixes it: by the volatile rule, the write happens-before every subsequent read."
@@s047 Type 6 · "Is it fair? Can a thread starve?"
@@s048 Exercises 2.1.2, 2.1.4, 4.1.4.
@@s049 Define: <b>starvation</b> = a thread that wants to enter never manages to, while others keep entering.
@@s050 Show the scenario: a continuous flow of readers that never lets the count drop to 0.
@@s051 Distinguish: the <b>solution</b> (for example the <code>writer</code> flag that blocks new readers) from the <b>guarantee of the lock</b> (non-fair lock: the choice of who enters is arbitrary). <a href="../teoria/l02-fairness.html">Fairness</a>.
@@s052 Words not to mix up
@@s053 Do not say
@@s054 When you mean
@@s055 Say instead
@@s056 race condition
@@s057 two conflicting accesses not ordered by HB
@@s058 <b>data race</b>
@@s059 data race
@@s060 the result depends on the order (count = 1)
@@s061 <b>race condition</b>
@@s062 synchronization action
@@s063 read or write of a variable, even volatile
@@s064 <b>variable access</b>
@@s065 "the lock makes it atomic"
@@s066 no other thread can execute the critical section at the same time
@@s067 <b>mutual exclusion</b> on the critical section
@@s068 "it works, I ran it"
@@s069 the program is correct
@@s070 an argument over <b>all</b> the interleavings / all the executions
@@s071 thread-safe (in a vague sense)
@@s072 no data race on the fields
@@s073 <b>thread-safe</b> according to the course definition
